{"message":"Twenty minutes on an early `return` is not a dumb-person problem - it's the most common way accumulator loops break.

The function hands back the first even number it finds and stops there. Look at where `return total` sits relative to the `for` - what does `return` do to a loop the moment it runs?

Move it out of the loop body and re-run. The totals should start accumulating properly.","hintLevel":2,"identifiedIssues":["`return total` is inside the loop, so the function exits after the first even number."],"suggestedNextStep":"Move `return total` outside the loop so it runs once, after every item is processed.","understandingCheck":"What happens to a `for` loop when a `return` statement executes inside it?","requiresExecution":true}

{"message":"It's remembering because that list isn't created per call. It's created once, when Python defines the function, and stored on the function object. Every call that doesn't supply a second argument gets that same list back.

The mental model to correct: `my_list=[]` does not mean "make a fresh list each time". Default values are evaluated at definition time and reused. Mutating one is therefore visible to every later call - which is why calling from two different places still accumulates.

So the real question isn't why it remembers, it's where the list should come from when the caller doesn't provide one. What default value could you use that isn't itself a container?","hintLevel":2,"identifiedIssues":["The default `[]` is one shared list created at function definition time.","Mutating it inside the function leaks state across unrelated calls."],"suggestedNextStep":"Call the function three times without a second argument and print each result to confirm the shared object.","understandingCheck":"Is `my_list=[]` evaluated when the function is defined, or when it is called?","requiresExecution":false}

{"message":"The recursion itself is fine. What's missing is the exit. Every call hands a smaller `n` to the next call and waits for an answer, but nothing ever says "stop, this is the answer".

Ask yourself: what is the smallest input for which multiplying by `factorial(...)` stops making sense? Naming that input tells you what condition your guard needs.

Don't write the branch yet. Tell me what the function should return at that smallest input, and why that value is mathematically forced rather than arbitrary.","hintLevel":1,"identifiedIssues":["There is no base case, so `factorial` recurses until the interpreter's recursion limit."],"suggestedNextStep":"Work out what `factorial` must return for `n = 0` and `n = 1` before writing any branch.","understandingCheck":"What should `factorial(0)` be, and what happens to the whole multiplication chain if it isn't that?","requiresExecution":true}

{"message":"It isn't meaningless, but it isn't evidence either. The pass is recorded as a completed exercise. What's absent is mastery: when you pass, the system credits mastery only for the concepts the exercise declares in `exercise_concepts`. This one declares none, so no mastery evidence is recorded and no review cards are scheduled.

Redoing it won't change anything - the same exercise with the same empty mapping produces the same result every time. And you can't change the mapping from the workspace; it's authored content attached to the exercise, not learner data, so there's nothing here for you to repair.

If what you want is mastery evidence, the useful question is which *mapped* exercises in this lesson assess the concept you're trying to prove. Lesson completion and review scheduling both work from the union of the concepts those exercises declare, so the mapped ones are the only places your evidence can come from.","hintLevel":null,"identifiedIssues":[],"suggestedNextStep":"Switch to a mapped exercise in the same lesson and produce the evidence there.","understandingCheck":"What's the difference between an exercise being passed and a concept being credited?","requiresExecution":false}

{"message":"In Python, `counts[word] += 1` evaluates `counts[word]` on the right-hand side first before storing anything back. If the key isn't in the dictionary yet, reading it raises `KeyError` immediately.

So before you can add 1, you need to decide what count a brand-new word starts with.

What are the two ways Python lets you check or provide a default for a key that might not exist yet?","hintLevel":1,"identifiedIssues":["`counts[word] += 1` reads `counts[word]` before it exists, raising a KeyError."],"suggestedNextStep":"Check `if word in counts:` before incrementing, or use `counts.get(word, 0)`.","understandingCheck":"Why does `counts['new'] = 1` succeed without an error, but `counts['new'] += 1` raises a KeyError?","requiresExecution":true}

